# Suppressions for reachable memory held by OpenSSL's own internal RSA/BN
# caches (RSA blinding state, BN_MONT_CTX Montgomery contexts, and similar
# lazily-computed values populated during RSA key generation and signing;
# exercised here by tests_parser's stream_tls test group, which uses
# ctls_ctx_cert_add()'s self-signed certificate generation to drive a real
# TLS handshake).
#
# These blocks are allocated via OpenSSL's own CRYPTO_malloc/CRYPTO_zalloc
# wrappers (or, depending on the exact libcrypto build, malloc/realloc
# called directly from a caller those wrappers were inlined into) and kept
# alive for the lifetime of the process by libcrypto's own internal
# static/thread-local state; they are only released by an explicit
# OPENSSL_cleanup() call, which this codebase does not make (calling it
# unconditionally at process exit is not safe in a library meant to be
# embedded in a larger application that may still have other OpenSSL
# handles in use). They are not leaks, matching the same category already
# documented in tests/ctls/openssl_internal.supp (this file's origin) and
# tests/chttpclient/ld_so_dlopen.supp (glibc's own NSS module loading).
#
# Matched by the allocation's own ORIGIN LIBRARY rather than a specific
# wrapper function name: whether CRYPTO_malloc/CRYPTO_zalloc/CRYPTO_strdup/
# CRYPTO_strndup/BN_MONT_CTX_new/OPENSSL_sk_deep_copy (and others) show up as
# their own separate frame between malloc/realloc and the rest of the stack,
# or get inlined away entirely, is a distro/compiler/optimization-level
# choice of however that particular libcrypto.so.3 was built, not something
# this codebase controls or can rely on being stable across environments;
# confirmed directly (same libcrypto version and test, different distro
# builds) to vary exactly this way. Any "still reachable" block whose
# backtrace includes at least one frame inside libcrypto.so.3 or
# libssl.so.3 is, by construction, memory this codebase's own code did not
# allocate and cannot safely free, regardless of which of OpenSSL's own many
# internal call paths produced it or how many of its own wrapper frames that
# particular build happened to inline away.
{
   openssl_internal_still_reachable_via_libcrypto
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   ...
   obj:*/libcrypto.so.3
   ...
}
{
   openssl_internal_still_reachable_via_libssl
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   ...
   obj:*/libssl.so.3
   ...
}
